From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 20:49:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] net: stmmac: dwmac-sun8i: No TX checksum insertion above
 MTU 1500

On an Allwinner H616, the EMAC inserts wrong checksums into frames
longer than 2032 bytes, and sometimes corrupts the frame sent after
such a frame. Measured with an earlier version of these patches, which
left checksum insertion on at MTU 4070, in MAC loopback:
- UDP frames of 2036 and 2040 bytes: 0 of 40 had valid checksums (the
  payload was intact);
- 2044-byte UDP frames checksummed in software, sent right after them:
  4 of 20 intact;
- 2054-byte raw frames: 1 of 30 had 2 bytes changed at offset 24.
UDP frames of 2032 bytes were fine; frames of 2033 to 2035 bytes were
not tried. With checksum insertion off, all of these frames were
intact. I have not found the cause, and only the H616 was measured.

Set bugged_jumbo, as the dwmac 3.40a, 3.610 and 3.710 cores do, so that
stmmac_fix_features() turns TX checksum offload off above MTU 1500, and
with it scatter-gather. All dwmac-sun8i variants get it, as they share
the EMAC design. TX stays in store-and-forward mode, which stmmac
selects from plat->tx_coe.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun8i.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun8i.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun8i.c
index 3de46f8..d89e694 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun8i.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/stmicro/stmmac/dwmac-sun8i.c
@@ -1261,6 +1261,8 @@ static int sun8i_dwmac_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	 */
 	plat_dat->rx_coe = STMMAC_RX_COE_TYPE2;
 	plat_dat->tx_coe = true;
+	/* TX checksum insertion corrupts frames longer than 2032 bytes */
+	plat_dat->bugged_jumbo = true;
 	plat_dat->flags |= STMMAC_FLAG_HAS_SUN8I;
 	plat_dat->bsp_priv = gmac;
 	plat_dat->init = sun8i_dwmac_init;
